  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM Thursday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Well-above-normal temperatures expected on Friday.\n\nRidging aloft will briefly build over the Midwest on Friday ahead of \na shortwave moving through the SW US.  A backdoor sold front will be \nto our north and poised to push through the area Friday night with \ncool high pressure to follow. Ahead of the front and downstream of \nthe ridging, thicknesses are correspondingly forecast to rise 20m \nfrom thus morning to Friday morning, suggesting upper 70s to lower \n80s.  Most MOS is a degree or two higher than the NBM, and will \nleave toward MOS in a slightly amplified pattern, with highs of 80-\n83.  These values were 10-15 degrees above normal but 2-3 degrees \nbelow record values.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Very light, non-accumulating rain, is possible \nSunday night into Monday. There is a much better chance for \naccumulating rainfall from mid to late next week.\n\nA shortwave associated with the remnants of Tropical Storm Vance \nwhich is currently just south of Baja California will amplify as it \nmoves NE into the Southern Plains on Saturday, then track into the \nSoutheast US on Sunday/Monday. While it will result in widespread \nmid-level cloud cover during this time, by the time the system gets \nthis far east, any precipitation looks to have a hard time reaching \nthe ground. This is due to fairly dry air in the low levels from \nsurface high pressure extending down the Eastern Seaboard behind \nFriday night's cold frontal passage. Deterministic and ensemble \nguidance has trended to a mostly dry solution, keeping the deeper \nmoisture pinned to near the Gulf Coast and FL. Only about 10-20% of \nECM/GFS/CMC ensemble members depict any measurable precipitation \nover central NC on Sunday night/Monday, and the ones that do are \nvery light. So POPs have again trended downward and are now below \nthe slight chance range.\n\nMeanwhile, a deep mid/upper trough is forecast to progress from the \nWestern US on Sunday to the MS Valley on Wednesday and Thursday, \nlikely closing off into a low as it does so. At the surface, a \ncyclone will develop and deepen over the Central Plains as a weaker \nsecondary low potentially develops over the Southern Plains and \nlower MS Valley. An associated strong cold front will move east \nacross these areas as well. As the high to our NE moves away during \nthis time, it will be replaced by deep moist southerly flow ahead of \nthe aforementioned frontal system. The timing and degree of phasing \nthat occurs between the northern and southern stream pieces of \nenergy over the Western US will have a big impact on the timing and \nstrength of the system as it moves east, and that is still highly \nuncertain at this time. However, ensemble guidance is in much better \nagreement that we will at least receive some precipitation from mid \nto late week, particularly on Thursday. Deterministic guidance is \ngenerally wetter than the ensembles, but even ensemble means \nindicate close to an inch of rain is possible on average. Severe \nweather can't be ruled out given the strong deep-layer flow, but \nstill too early to pinpoint exactly where that will be, and \ninstability looks fairly marginal at this time.",
